ach odd m from 1 to 99, as in m=1,3,...,99, each such m gives a chain.\n\nBut when considering these chains, a number like 6 is in chains for m=1 and m=3. Therefore, the union of these chains covers the entire set. However, the chains overlap. So, the question is, how many chains do we need to cover the set? If each number is in exactly one chain, then the total number of chains required would be equal to the number of equivalence classes under the relation a ~ b iff a divides b and b divides a (i.e., a = b). So, in other words, the number of prime power equivalence classes? Wait, not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the number of chains needed corresponds to the number of distinct prime factors. For example, primes, prime squares, etc. But this is unclear.\n\nAlternatively, think of the problem in terms of the following. The maximum antichain in the divisibility poset is equal to the number of elements in the set that are square-free. Because square-free numbers cannot be divided by each other\'s squares. Wait, but then, if we take all square-free numbers, but square-free numbers can still have a divisor relation. For example, 8 and 6 are both square-free? No, 6=2*3, and 8=2^3. 8 is square-free, 6 is also square-free. But 8 divides 6? No. So, square-free numbers can coexist in an antichain. Wait, but 4 is square-free? No, because it\'s 2^2. So if we take all square-free numbers, the antichain size would be 21 (as per the count of square-free numbers up to 100). But 21 is much less than 25 or 7.\n\nAlternatively, the maximum antichain might be the largest subset of numbers where no two have a divisor relation. This is equivalent to the largest set of numbers such that none divides the other. The standard approach for this is to use the principle of inclusion-exclusion or dynamic programming, but for a general N.\n\nHowever, for N=100, perhaps constructing the antichain by including the smallest element from each chain. But since each chain can have up to 7 elements, th